WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.245
The cost and latency case: a small model that replaces a big one.

2
00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:09.581
The strongest business reason to fine-tune is often not quality but cost and speed.

3
00:00:09.861 --> 00:00:17.008
Suppose your extraction feature works well with a large frontier model and a long prompt full of instructions and examples.

4
00:00:17.288 --> 00:00:22.323
Every request pays for those prompt tokens, and a large model is slower per token.

5
00:00:22.603 --> 00:00:31.221
A small model fine-tuned on a few thousand examples of the same task may need no examples in the prompt and can run on modest hardware.

6
00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:43.000
OpenAI’s optimization guide names both effects: shorter prompts save tokens and reduce latency, and a smaller model trained for a specific task can be cheaper and faster.

7
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:54.309
Its fine-tuning guide describes distillation: tune a prompt on a large model, use its outputs (checked) as training data, and fine-tune a smaller model to do the same task.

8
00:00:54.589 --> 00:01:07.133
The savings are real only above some volume, and teams routinely leave costs out of the comparison: Building the dataset: collecting, labeling, reviewing, deduplicating (lessons 5 and 6).

9
00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:09.419
This is usually the largest cost.

10
00:01:09.699 --> 00:01:12.984
Training and evaluation runs, including the failed ones.

11
00:01:13.264 --> 00:01:18.171
Serving: if you host the model, you pay for GPUs whether or not traffic arrives.

12
00:01:18.451 --> 00:01:25.363
Maintenance: when the base model is retired, your fine-tuned model goes with it, and you redo the work on a new base.

13
00:01:25.643 --> 00:01:27.819
That last point is not hypothetical.

14
00:01:28.099 --> 00:01:31.747
Amazon Bedrock offered fine-tuning for Claude 3 Haiku.

15
00:01:32.027 --> 00:01:48.795
Bedrock’s lifecycle page shows that model entering Legacy status on March 10, 2026, after which no new fine-tuning jobs can be created, and reaching end of life on September 10, 2026 in the Region where fine-tuning ran.

16
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:56.584
OpenAI’s docs likewise say fine-tuned models stay available for inference until their base model is deprecated.

17
00:01:56.864 --> 00:02:01.408
A fine-tuned model is a depreciating asset with a known maximum life.

18
00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:02.733
Price that in.
